// with Agents" (Cusati & Brown). Source repository: djjay0131/ai-empirical-se-chapter
// (private until the chapter is accepted). The goals paragraph below and the
// proposal page are the final version of 23 October 2026.
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import lifecycle from "../../../data/governed-agentic-research/ai-in-empirical-se/sources.json";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const research = `${base}research/`;
const soa = `${base}research/soa-agentic-se/`;
const proposal = `${base}research/governed-agentic-research/proposal/`;
const pdf = `${proposal}cusati-brown-chapter-proposal-2026-10-23.pdf`;
---

<Base
  annotatable
  title="Jason Cusati — Governed Agentic Empirical Research"
  description="From Papers to Progress, with Agents — a Springer book-chapter proposal by Jason Cusati and Chris Brown arguing that once AI agents participate in empirical software engineering research, the research process itself must be governed. The proposal and the research behind it."
>
  <p class="crumbs"><a href={research}>← Research</a></p>
  <h1>From Papers to Progress, with Agents</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Governing AI-assisted empirical software engineering research · Jason Cusati and Chris Brown, Virginia Tech</p>
  <p class="lede">
    A chapter proposal for the Springer edited volume <em>Using Artificial
    Intelligence in Empirical Software Engineering Research</em> (eds. Mäntylä,
    Mendez, Franch, Kalinowski). Proposal due 23 October 2026; chapter, if
    accepted, through June 2027.
  </p>

  <h2>The argument <span class="badge">proposal · final · 23 October 2026</span></h2>
  <div class="abstract">
    <p>
      AI is no longer a tool a researcher calls at one step of a study; in
      empirical software engineering it now plans, retrieves, extracts and
      critiques, with decisions delegated to it along the way. Current guidance
      asks authors to disclose models and prompts, keep session traces and
      validate AI output by hand, and stage-specific frameworks now govern
      literature screening and repository mining. What no standard yet
      specifies is the research <em>process</em> as a governed object: which
      decisions were delegated to AI and under what authority, which
      intermediate artifacts they produced, and which human checks accepted
      them, recorded as reviewable provenance across the whole study. This is
      the loss of provenance already known to limit knowledge accumulation in
      software engineering, now inside the study and at machine speed — and the
      same agents can make the remedy affordable.
    </p>
    <p>
      The chapter operationalizes four published principles for research
      artifacts — structured, inspectable, long-lived, governed (Cusati and
      Brown, ICSE 2026 Future of Software Engineering) — for AI-assisted
      empirical research: agents work under bounded authority, claims are
      recorded as typed entities, decisions leave durable provenance, and
      reproducibility is built in as executable checks. It is grounded in
      working tooling and two worked cases, presented as an experience report
      and reference implementation rather than a validated method.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="note">
    From the final proposal of 23 October 2026. <a href={proposal}>Read the
    full one-page proposal →</a> or <a href={pdf}>download the PDF</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Proposal and research</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <a class="card" href={proposal}>
      <span class="card-title">The chapter proposal</span>
      <span class="card-desc">The one-page proposal in the five parts the editors asked for: authors, category, goals and audience, a six-part chapter outline, and the supplementary material we will release.</span>
      <span class="card-meta">Final · 23 October 2026 · PDF available</span>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href={`${base}research/governed-agentic-research/ai-in-empirical-se/`}>
      <span class="card-title">AI and agents across the empirical-SE lifecycle</span>
      <span class="card-desc">What has been published on LLMs and agents at each stage of empirical SE research — study design, literature reviews, repository mining, qualitative and quantitative analysis, artifact generation, reporting — and what it says about rigor, validity, reproducibility and the human role. Anchored on Gomes et al. (2026).</span>
      <span class="card-meta">{lifecycle.length} sources · 6 October 2026 · synthesis, source explorer, Consensus searches, literature-gap table</span>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>How this relates to the other projects</h2>
  <p>
    The chapter is the methodological layer on top of work that is reported
    elsewhere on this site. Each project below supplies one strand of the
    argument; none of them is the chapter.
  </p>
  <ul class="relations">
    <li>
      <a href={`${soa}agentic-harnesses/`}>Agentic harnesses</a> and
      <a href={`${soa}agentic-memory/`}>Agent memory</a> (the
      <a href={soa}>State of the Art in Agentic SE</a> project) are themselves
      governed research passes: schema-constrained source records, discovery
      separated from an adversarially prompted verification pass, provenance
      fields on every record. They are the method's literature-review stage in
      practice — one stage of the lifecycle, not the whole process.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Research.AI</strong> (the <code>agentic-kg</code> project under
      the knowledge-graphs tenet) supplies the chapter's sharpest case for human
      methodological judgement: reconciling a gold-label schema where the
      labelling choice itself could distort measured precision and recall, so
      the evaluation would measure the researchers' decision rather than the
      system's behaviour.
    </li>
    <li>
      A time-boxed empirical study run in August 2026 under the framework's
      tooling (<code>agentic-governance</code>, <code>agentic-research</code>)
      is one of the proposal's two worked cases: typed claims, pre-registered
      thresholds, a human re-derivation ledger and an adversarial reviewer
      gating what could be reported. Changes that came out of it flowed back
      into both tools.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>
    The one-page proposal was finalized for the 23 October 2026 deadline;
    notification is expected on 6 November. The
    chapter repository stays private until the chapter is accepted.
  </p>
</Base>

